-- Version 17 → 18: watching a forum, and watching a post.
--
-- Two new tables and nothing else. No table is rebuilt and no column is added,
-- which makes this the smallest migration since 12.sqm — the whole of LNL-63's
-- storage is two join tables whose presence-of-a-row *is* the subscription, and
-- there is nowhere else in the schema for a watch to be recorded.
--
-- The dual-path story is 1.sqm's and 2.sqm's: a fresh volume runs `CREATE TABLE`
-- out of Subscriptions.sq and gets both of these for free; a volume already at
-- version 17 runs this instead. MigrationTest migrates every earlier snapshot
-- forward and compares PRAGMA output against a fresh `Schema.create`, so drift
-- between the two files fails the build rather than the volume.
--
-- ── Order does not matter here, unlike 15.sqm and 16.sqm ────────────────────
--
-- Both tables point at things that already exist — `users`, `forums`,
-- `forum_posts` — and neither points at the other. Stated rather than left
-- unsaid because the two migrations before this one both had an order that was
-- load-bearing and invisible, and "this one does not" is worth being able to
-- read rather than having to re-derive.
--
-- ── What existing rows get: nothing, because there are none ─────────────────
--
-- Neither table is backfilled and there is no backfill that would be correct.
-- Watching is something somebody asks for, so a volume that has been running
-- since before this migration has nobody watching a forum — which is exactly
-- what an empty table says. The one automatic subscription in the feature (a
-- post's author, at publish) is written by the publish route from that point on
-- and is deliberately not applied retroactively: subscribing somebody to a
-- thread they wrote three months ago, without asking, would mail them about a
-- conversation they had already stopped following.
--
-- The statements below reproduce Subscriptions.sq's two new CREATE TABLE blocks,
-- minus the prose. See that file for what each constraint is for.

CREATE TABLE forum_new_post_subscriptions (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    forum_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, forum_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (forum_id) REFERENCES forums(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

CREATE TABLE forum_post_subscriptions (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    post_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, post_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (post_id) REFERENCES forum_posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);
